From e8718a05cca37482f2bed46b4c0cb62fb4035b0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 05:57:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/44] feat(chexc): infer statics and by effective visibility,
 re-checking inferred overrides

Two narrowings existed for one reason: an override's list is compared against the
overridden declaration's during type check, which runs before capability parameter
inference, so a list acquired BY inference was never compared at all. Non-private
static members therefore required an explicit clause, and eligibility used the
declaration's own modifier instead of effective visibility.

The pairs are now recorded where the inheritance checker already has them and
consumed after inference: an inferred override requiring more than what it overrides
is an error, with the overridden declaration pointed at. A list depends on its own
body only, never on its overriders.

With that in place both narrowings go. Static members are ordinary members, and
visibility is effective visibility -- the narrowest of a declaration's own modifier
and those of its enclosing declarations -- so a 'public' method of an 'internal'
class is inferred, as is a 'private init' of a 'public' class, while anything that
really leaves the module keeps an authoritative list.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
